<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis XII of France</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1462">1462</a>–<a href="/years/1515">1515</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Father of the People · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-valois">House of Valois</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis XII, born at Blois as duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>, became king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1498. He had his first marriage annulled to marry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anne-of-brittany">Anne of Brittany</a>, widow of Charles VIII, keeping Brittany tied to the crown. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-wars">Italian Wars</a> he conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> in 1499 and briefly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentinois">Valentinois</a> a duchy for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-borgia">Cesare Borgia</a>. Popular for low taxes, he was called the Father of the People.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-xii-of-france.jpg" alt="Louis XII of France" width="320" height="418" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Workshop of Jean Perréal · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ludwig_XII._von_Frankreich.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Louis of Orléans was born on 27 June 1462 at the Château de Blois, the son of Charles I, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>, who was almost seventy, and Marie of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kleve">Cleves</a>. He became Duke of Orléans at the age of three. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xi">Louis XI</a> distrusted the Orléanist link with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a> and opposed the idea of an Orléanist ever reaching the throne. In 1476 he forced Louis, his second cousin, to marry his own disabled and supposedly sterile daughter Joan, hoping to extinguish the Orléans cadet branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-valois">House of Valois</a>.</p><p>Louis rose against the regency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anne-of-france">Anne of France</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mad-war">Mad War</a> of 1485 to 1488. Allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Francis II</a>, Duke of Brittany, he was defeated and captured at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-saint-aubin-du-cormier">Battle of Saint-Aubin-du-Cormier</a> on 28 July 1488. Pardoned three years later, he joined King Charles VIII in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, leading the vanguards. At Fornovo on 6 July 1495 he was left in a weak position when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludovico-sforza">Ludovico Sforza</a> changed sides, and he came to hate Sforza.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>All four children of Charles VIII and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anne-of-brittany">Anne of Brittany</a> died in infancy. Louis, great-grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, was the senior claimant under the Salic Law, and he became king on 7 April 1498. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-vi">Pope Alexander VI</a> annulled his marriage with Joan in December 1498, and Louis married Anne of Brittany, which kept Brittany tied to the crown. He reformed the legal system and reduced taxes, paying for it by cutting the pensions of the nobility and of foreign princes. The Ordinance of Blois of 1499 codified French customary law, and the Ordinance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> of 1510 tightened tax collection.</p><p>He claimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> through his grandmother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentina-visconti">Valentina Visconti</a>. In 1501 he sent an army to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, which a secret treaty signed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grenada">Grenada</a> on 11 November 1500 had divided between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, but he was forced to cede it to Spain in 1504. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-league-of-cambrai">War of the League of Cambrai</a> he defeated the Venetians at Agnadello on 14 May 1509, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii">Pope Julius II</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-league-1511">Holy League</a> against him, and the Swiss drove the French from Milan in 1513.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The Estates conferred on Louis the title Father of the People in 1506, the only time a French king received it. Historians often attribute his popularity to his tax policy. He died on 1 January 1515, after suffering from gout, without a male heir, and was buried at Saint Denis. Francis I, his cousin and son-in-law, succeeded him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Louis XII” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When Charles died in 1498 she resumed rule of her duchy and married his successor <b>Louis XII</b> in 1499 under a contract meant to keep Brittany separate.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brittany">Duchy of Brittany · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The French invasions changed everything: <b>Louis XII</b>, claiming the duchy through his grandmother Valentina Visconti, conquered it in 1499, and Ludovico Sforza was captured at Novara in 1500.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Charles VIII of France entered the city in February 1495 almost unopposed but soon withdrew; <b>Louis XII</b> and Ferdinand II of Aragon agreed in 1500 to partition the kingdom, then fell out, and the Spanish commander Gonzalo Fernández de Córdoba, the 'Great Captain', won the Battle of Cerignola,…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The same day King <b>Louis XII of France</b> named him Duke of Valentinois, a title chosen as homophonous with his nickname Il Valentino.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-17">17 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Pope Julius II, <b>Louis XII of France</b>, Emperor Maximilian I and Ferdinand of Aragon formally concluded the League of Cambrai against Venice.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-10">10 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In the secret Treaty of Granada, <b>Louis XII of France</b> and Ferdinand II of Aragon agreed to partition the Kingdom of Naples.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-11">11 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Château de Blois, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Hôtel des Tournelles, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.59,1.33&amp;year=1496" aria-label="Château de Blois on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4581 496 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Louis XII of France"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5031 766h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5031 766h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.59,1.33&amp;year=1496">What was here in 1496? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
